Phase 1: Preparation (Set the Stage)
Install: Install and set up your MongoDB Server.

Environment: Create a Python virtual environment and install necessary libraries: pymongo, pandas, textblob, and flask.

Phase 2: Data Ingestion & Preprocessing (The Big Data Store)
Write Script: Create a Python script to read the amazon Food Reviews 100k Dataset.csv file.

Calculate: For every review, use textblob to calculate the sentiment_score and Python's len() to calculate content_length.

Load: Use collection.insert_many() in pymongo to insert all 100,000 processed documents into your MongoDB database.

Phase 3: MongoDB Aggregation (The Core Analysis)
Develop Pipelines: Write the three complex Aggregation Pipelines using the MongoDB query language. These pipelines will process the 100,000 documents to produce summarized, small JSON results.

Pipeline 1: Sentiment by Rating (Grouping → Average Sentiment).

Pipeline 2: Content Length vs. Rating (Bucketing by Length → Average Rating).

Pipeline 3: Rating Distribution by Sentiment (Bucketing by Sentiment → Count Distribution).

Phase 4: Application Backend (The Bridge)
Flask App: Set up a basic Flask web application.

API Endpoints: Define three API routes (e.g., /api/sentiment_by_rating).

Execute: Have each route execute one of the Aggregation Pipelines from Phase 3 and return the resulting summarized data as a JSON response.

Phase 5: Visualization (Analyzing the Results)
Dashboard: Create a single HTML page (your website dashboard).

Fetch Data: Use JavaScript to call the three API endpoints you created in Flask.

Render Charts: Use Chart.js (or similar library) to display the analysis results visually.

🎨 How the Website Dashboard Should Look
Your final website should be a simple Analytics Dashboard that clearly presents the results of your three MongoDB analyses.

The key to meeting the "analyzing the results" requirement is to have a clean, informative layout, typically with three main sections:

1. Header and Data Summary
Title: "Amazon Food Review Analysis Dashboard"

Metrics: A small box showing the total number of reviews analyzed (100,000), proving you handled the "big data."

2. Chart 1: Sentiment by Engagement (Bar Chart)
Title: Average Sentiment Score by Star Rating

Visualization: A Bar Chart with 5 bars (for 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 stars).

Insight: Visually shows whether a 5-star review always has the highest average positive sentiment score (closest to +1.0) and a 1-star review is always closest to -1.0.

3. Chart 2: Content Length vs. Engagement (Bar Chart)
Title: Average Rating by Review Length Category

Visualization: A Bar Chart with three bars: "Short," "Medium," and "Long" (based on your $bucket definitions).

Insight: Shows whether reviewers tend to write longer reviews when they are very happy or very angry, or if they write the shortest reviews for average ratings.

4. Chart 3: Rating Distribution by Sentiment (Stacked Bar Chart)
Title: Star Rating Distribution Within Sentiment Groups

Visualization: A Stacked Bar Chart where the main bars are "Negative," "Neutral," and "Positive." Each bar is segmented to show the breakdown of 1-star, 2-star, etc., ratings within that sentiment group.

Insight: Demonstrates, for example, what percentage of "Positive" reviews were actually 4-stars versus 5-stars, revealing nuances in the data.
